<p>Additional turnkey firms worth shortlisting alongside boutique studios: <strong>Livspace</strong> (structured, technology-led, 45-day modular handover), <strong>DesignCafe</strong> (Banjara Hills experience centre, wide package range) and <strong>Decorpot</strong> (ten-year warranty, factory-direct execution). Each of these is a serious option if warranty and process are more important to you than design signature.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Which category is right for you?</span><strong>Choose a boutique studio</strong> if you want a signature outcome, are willing to invest time in the design phase, and are budgeting design fees on top of execution.<br><br><strong>Choose a luxury specialist</strong> if you want bespoke design with a single accountable team and a defined process — the mid-point between boutique and turnkey.<br><br><strong>Choose a warranted turnkey firm</strong> if predictability of budget, timeline and warranty matter more to you than design distinctiveness.</div>
<h2>Neighborhood-wise strengths</h2>
<p>Hyderabad's premium interior market is unusually concentrated geographically. Most firms cluster their coverage in the western and south-western belt, and different studios have different natural strengths by neighborhood.</p>
<h4>Jubilee Hills, Banjara Hills</h4>
<p>Villa and legacy-home territory. Boutique studios and luxury specialists dominate here — the material expectations are high and clients are typically comparing three or four studios before signing. Strongest options: Urban Zen, De Panache, Tint Tone and Shade, Morph Design Co.</p>
<h4>Gachibowli, Financial District, Kokapet</h4>
<p>High-rise penthouse and premium apartment territory, plus newer gated villa communities. Turnkey firms and luxury specialists both work here — the buyer profile skews IT-executive and returning-NRI, and the design language leans contemporary. Strongest options: The Koncept House, HomeGlanza, Studio Inscape for new villas.</p>
<h4>Tellapur, Narsingi, Manikonda</h4>
<p>Emerging premium villa communities. Studios comfortable with both design and light civil work — kitchen expansions, false ceiling rework, wet area upgrades — do best. Studio Inscape and Spacefiction Studio are natural fits given the frequency of structural touch-ups needed.</p>
<h4>HITEC City, Kondapur, Madhapur</h4>
<p>Established premium apartment stock, often 3BHK and 4BHK. Turnkey firms with strong modular systems and quick handover cycles are most efficient here — Livspace, DesignCafe and Decorpot have systematised for this segment.</p>
<h2>Real 2026 cost benchmarks for premium interiors</h2>
<p>The single most useful piece of information a homeowner can carry into a discovery call is a realistic price band. Here's what premium interiors actually cost in Hyderabad, based on scope rather than headline packaging.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Scope tier</th><th>Per sq ft</th><th>2BHK turnkey</th><th>3BHK turnkey</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr><td>Entry premium</td><td>₹1,500 – ₹2,000</td><td>₹9 – ₹13 lakh</td><td>₹14 – ₹20 lakh</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Mid premium</td><td>₹2,000 – ₹3,000</td><td>₹13 – ₹20 lakh</td><td>₹20 – ₹32 lakh</td></tr>
    <tr><td>High premium</td><td>₹3,000 – ₹4,500</td><td>₹20 – ₹28 lakh</td><td>₹32 – ₹45 lakh</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Ultra luxury</td><td>₹4,500 – ₹8,000+</td><td>₹28 lakh+</td><td>₹45 – ₹80 lakh+</td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Ranges include false ceilings, veneer joinery, hardware, modular kitchen, wardrobes, lighting, painting, wallpaper where used, and basic soft furnishing. They exclude civil work, imported stone, home automation and free-standing designer furniture, which typically add 15–30% at the high end. Boutique architect-led studios charge a design fee of 8–15% on top of the execution number.</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Watch for</span>Any quote that offers a "premium" or "luxury" project at under ₹1,500 per sq ft is quietly downgrading materials. The visible finishes may look right in a render; the invisible ones — plywood grade, hardware brand, edge banding — will be where the cost has been taken out. Ask for a line-item bill of materials before signing anything.</div>
<h2>The buyer's shortlisting framework</h2>
<p>A practical five-step framework we recommend clients follow before signing any premium interior contract in Hyderabad.</p>
<h4>1. Shortlist by category, not by ranking</h4>
<p>Decide whether you want a boutique studio, a luxury specialist or a turnkey firm before you compare portfolios. Comparing across categories is comparing different products.</p>
<h4>2. Visit one completed project per shortlisted firm</h4>
<p>Renders and photographs are unreliable indicators of build quality. A ninety-minute site visit to a two-year-old project tells you more than a fifteen-page portfolio. Look at edge banding on wardrobes, hardware silence, veneer joins and lighting temperature consistency.</p>
<h4>3. Ask for a fixed-quote estimate with brand and grade line items</h4>
<p>Any credible firm will name specific plywood grades (710 BWP for wet areas, BWR for dry), hardware brands (Blum, Hettich or equivalent), veneer suppliers and stone sources. A quote that reads "premium plywood, quality hardware" is a placeholder, not a commitment.</p>
<h4>4. Insist on a 3D walkthrough before signing execution</h4>
<p>Modern design software makes this trivial to produce; a studio that resists is either understaffed or hoping you'll approve unresolved decisions. Walk the entire home in 3D, verify sight lines, and confirm every fixed element.</p>
<h4>5. Confirm warranty terms in writing</h4>
<p>Ask specifically what the warranty covers, what voids it, and whether service is handled by the firm directly or routed through a channel partner. A five-year written warranty that requires the original invoice and rules out "cosmetic damage" is meaningfully different from a five-year warranty with a clear service commitment.</p>
<h2>Material specification checklist</h2>
<p>A premium quote at any price tier should specify the following. If it doesn't, ask.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Plywood.</strong> 710-grade BWP in kitchens and wet areas, BWR in wardrobes and living. Named brand — CenturyPly, Greenply, Kitply or equivalent.</li>
  <li><strong>Veneer or laminate.</strong> Engineered veneer thickness (0.5mm+), sheet manufacturer, and finishing method (PU matte, PU high gloss, wax). For laminate, brand and grade (1mm+ for shutters).</li>
  <li><strong>Hardware.</strong> Hinges, drawer channels and lift-up mechanisms by brand (Blum, Hettich, Hafele). Load ratings on drawer channels.</li>
  <li><strong>Handles and pulls.</strong> Named brand and finish. Handleless designs should specify the profile system.</li>
  <li><strong>Modular kitchen carcass.</strong> Full BWP or MR-grade plywood, edge-banded on all four sides, 18mm minimum.</li>
  <li><strong>Countertops.</strong> Engineered stone (Caesarstone, Silestone, Kalinga Stone) or natural stone — with slab thickness and edge profile.</li>
  <li><strong>False ceiling.</strong> Gypsum board brand (Saint-Gobain, USG Boral), section spacing and finish paint grade.</li>
  <li><strong>Lighting.</strong> Named fixture brands, colour temperature (2700K–3000K for living, 4000K for kitchen task), CRI 90+ for premium.</li>
  <li><strong>Painting.</strong> Brand and finish grade — for premium, expect Asian Paints Royale Luxury, Berger Silk or equivalent.</li>
  <li><strong>Wallpaper.</strong> Named manufacturer, roll dimensions, and adhesive specification.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">A quiet indicator of a serious firm</span>Ask the designer during your first meeting: "What's the plywood grade you use in wet areas by default?" A credible answer is a specific product name and a reason. An answer along the lines of "premium quality" is a signal.</div>
